{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:15149599 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:15149599 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"15149599","source":"MED","pmid":"15149599","doi":"10.1016/s1097-2765(04)00256-4","title":"Telomere shortening triggers senescence of human cells through a pathway involving ATM, p53, and p21(CIP1), but not p16(INK4a).","authorString":"Herbig U, Jobling WA, Chen BP, Chen DJ, Sedivy JM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Herbig U","lastName":"Herbig","firstName":"Utz","initials":"U","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6917-535X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Cell Biology and Biochemistry, Brown University, Providence, RI 02912, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Jobling WA","lastName":"Jobling","firstName":"Wendy A","initials":"WA"},{"fullName":"Chen BP","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Benjamin P C","initials":"BP"},{"fullName":"Chen DJ","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"David J","initials":"DJ"},{"fullName":"Sedivy JM","lastName":"Sedivy","firstName":"John M","initials":"JM"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6917-535X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"14","journalIssueId":1150471,"dateOfPublication":"2004 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2004,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Cell","title":"Molecular cell","essn":"1097-4164","issn":"1097-2765","nlmid":"9802571","isoabbreviation":"Mol Cell"},"printPublicationDate":"2004-05-01"},"pubYear":"2004","pageInfo":"501-513","abstractText":"Cellular senescence can be triggered by telomere shortening as well as a variety of stresses and signaling imbalances. We used multiparameter single-cell detection methods to investigate upstream signaling pathways and ensuing cell cycle checkpoint responses in human fibroblasts. Telomeric foci containing multiple DNA damage response factors were assembled in a subset of senescent cells and signaled through ATM to p53, upregulating p21 and causing G1 phase arrest. Inhibition of ATM expression or activity resulted in cell cycle reentry, indicating that stable arrest requires continuous signaling. ATR kinase appears to play a minor role in normal cells but in the absence of ATM elicited a delayed G2 phase arrest. These pathways do not affect expression of p16, which was upregulated in a telomere- and DNA damage-independent manner in a subset of cells. Distinct senescence programs can thus progress in parallel, resulting in mosaic cultures as well as individual cells responding to multiple signals.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology, Cell Biology and Biochemistry, Brown University, Providence, RI 02912, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCRR NIH HHS","acronym":"RR","grantId":"P20 RR-15578","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIA NIH HHS","acronym":"AG","grantId":"R01 AG16694","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Israel National Road Safety Authority","grantId":"R01 CA50519","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Israel National Road Safety Authority","grantId":"T32 GM-07601","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIA NIH HHS","acronym":"AG","grantId":"R01 AG18949","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA50519","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"T32 GM-07601","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Israel National Road Safety Authority","grantId":"F32 CA099388","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"F32 CA099388","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Israel National Road Safety Authority","grantId":"P20 RR-15578","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"P20 RR-15578","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Israel National Road Safety Authority","grantId":"R01 AG16